{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And he said.</b> I have already explained that every נא in Scripture is like עתה, and similarly דבר נא באזני העם (below 11:2), שמע נא יהושע (Zechariah 3:8), יומת נא את האיש הזה (Jeremiah 38:4), אוי נא לנו כי חטאנו (Lamentations 5:16)." "<b>By the hand of the one you will send.</b> Elided אשר, as if it were written ביד אשר תשלח, like עם לבבם שלם (II Chronicles 16:9). The meaning is that he should send this mission by the hand of Aaron his brother, who is a man of words, for he is not heavy of tongue, and he is accustomed to prophesy to Israel, and it is written in Ezekiel that HaShem sent a prophet in Israel before the coming of Moses our master, and it is written ויאמר ד׳ אל אהרן לך לקראת משה המדברה (below 20:27), and moreover he is older than him in years; perhaps it would be displeasing to him that Moses should be the emissary to Pharaoh—therefore HaShem said וראך ושמח בלבו (verse 14)." "Now Moses is not refusing to go; rather, at first he said לא איש דברים אנכי, for it is difficult for one who is heavy of mouth to speak before the king. He thought that HaShem would renew for him a sign to remove the heaviness of mouth, and when HaShem answered him והוריתיך, he knew that the heaviness of his tongue would not be removed. Now Moses was puzzled as to why HaShem would not send Aaron to Pharaoh, and because of this he said שלח נא ביד תשלח; and from HaShem's answer הלא אהרן אחיך הלוי all this is made clear to us."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:31:34.653645937Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}